    {
      "id": "Jej3Qz7p4DHKjAmjjwh45",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2339.579191265284,
      "y": 13.00721098125166,
      "width": 1430.963623046875,
      "height": 4185,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bFT",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1310110148,
      "version": 22,
      "versionNonce": 370282052,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1743069384064,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Rate limit:\n\n1. Token Bucket\nMaintains a bucket with N tokens refilled at a constant rate.\n\n\nEach request consumes a token; if empty, request is blocked.\n\n\nPros: Handles bursts well, provides smooth limiting.\n\n\nCons: Requires per-user token storage, needs logic for token refilling.\n\n\nBest for: APIs that need controlled bursts with steady limits.\n\n2. Leaky Bucket\nUses a queue to process requests at a fixed rate.\n\n\nNew requests are added to the queue, processed at a steady pace.\n\n\nPros: Prevents bursts, enforces consistent request flow.\n\n\nCons: Can delay requests, doesn't handle sudden high load efficiently.\n\n\nBest for: Systems needing smooth request processing over time.\n3. Fixed Window Counter\nMaintains a counter per time window (e.g., per minute).\n\n\nResets at fixed intervals (e.g., 2:55 → 2:56).\n\n\nPros: Simple, fast, low memory usage.\n\n\nCons: Allows burst requests at window boundaries.\n\n\nBest for: Simple rate limiting with predictable intervals.\n\n\n4. Sliding Window Log\nLogs timestamps of each request and counts entries within the last time window.\n\n\nPros: Precise rate limiting, smooth traffic handling.\n\n\nCons: High memory usage (stores all timestamps), expensive queries.\n\n\nBest for: Highly accurate request rate tracking.\n\n\n5. Sliding Window Counter\nStores only two counters: previous (P) and current (C) window.\n\n\nDecays P based on elapsed time to smooth transitions.\n\n\nPros: Memory-efficient, prevents bursts.\n\n\nCons: Computationally heavier than Fixed Window, approximate counting.\n\n\nBest for: Smoothed rate limiting with efficient storage.\n\n\n\n\nChoosing the Right Algorithm:\nFor simple limits → Fixed Window Counter.\n\n\nFor high accuracy → Sliding Window Log.\n\n\nFor efficient memory & smooth flow → Sliding Window Counter.\n\n\nFor burst handling → Token Bucket.\n\n\nFor steady request processing → Leaky Bucket.\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Rate limit:\n\n1. Token Bucket\nMaintains a bucket with N tokens refilled at a constant rate.\n\n\nEach request consumes a token; if empty, request is blocked.\n\n\nPros: Handles bursts well, provides smooth limiting.\n\n\nCons: Requires per-user token storage, needs logic for token refilling.\n\n\nBest for: APIs that need controlled bursts with steady limits.\n\n2. Leaky Bucket\nUses a queue to process requests at a fixed rate.\n\n\nNew requests are added to the queue, processed at a steady pace.\n\n\nPros: Prevents bursts, enforces consistent request flow.\n\n\nCons: Can delay requests, doesn't handle sudden high load efficiently.\n\n\nBest for: Systems needing smooth request processing over time.\n3. Fixed Window Counter\nMaintains a counter per time window (e.g., per minute).\n\n\nResets at fixed intervals (e.g., 2:55 → 2:56).\n\n\nPros: Simple, fast, low memory usage.\n\n\nCons: Allows burst requests at window boundaries.\n\n\nBest for: Simple rate limiting with predictable intervals.\n\n\n4. Sliding Window Log\nLogs timestamps of each request and counts entries within the last time window.\n\n\nPros: Precise rate limiting, smooth traffic handling.\n\n\nCons: High memory usage (stores all timestamps), expensive queries.\n\n\nBest for: Highly accurate request rate tracking.\n\n\n5. Sliding Window Counter\nStores only two counters: previous (P) and current (C) window.\n\n\nDecays P based on elapsed time to smooth transitions.\n\n\nPros: Memory-efficient, prevents bursts.\n\n\nCons: Computationally heavier than Fixed Window, approximate counting.\n\n\nBest for: Smoothed rate limiting with efficient storage.\n\n\n\n\nChoosing the Right Algorithm:\nFor simple limits → Fixed Window Counter.\n\n\nFor high accuracy → Sliding Window Log.\n\n\nFor efficient memory & smooth flow → Sliding Window Counter.\n\n\nFor burst handling → Token Bucket.\n\n\nFor steady request processing → Leaky Bucket.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
